\begin{note}
Many programming languages take a comparison function \lstinline{cmp} instead of a key function for determining sort order.
The \lstinline{cmp} function takes two parameters $a$ and $b$ and determines the sort order as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item A negative return value means $a<b$.
\item A zero return value means $a==b$.
\item A positive return value means $a>b$.
\end{enumerate}
In python, we prefer using the \lstinline{key} function when possible because python uses only $\Theta(n)$ calls to the key function,
whereas a full $O(n\log n)$ calls are required to the \lstinline{cmp} function.
Sometimes, however, it is easier to use the \lstinline{cmp} function,
and the build-int \lstinline{functools.cmp_to_key} converts the \lstinline{cmp} function to a \lstinline{key} that python's sorts can use.
\end{note}

